    <body>
      <sec id="article-79614.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Elevated Intracranial Pressure (ICP) represents a common and potentially life-threatening complication in patients with critical illness, acute trauma, intracranial hemorrhage, hydrocephalus, or cerebral edema. Risk factors include traumatic head injury, central nervous system infections, space-occupying lesions, and impaired cerebrospinal fluid outflow. Clinically, patients may present with headache, nausea, vomiting, altered mental status, papilledema, or hemodynamic instability. Prompt recognition and management are essential to prevent secondary brain injury, herniation, permanent neurologic deficits, or death. Noninvasive assessment using optic nerve sheath ultrasound (ONSUS) enables real-time evaluation of optic nerve sheath diameter, providing a rapid bedside estimation of ICP when invasive monitoring is unavailable, contraindicated, or delayed. Technical challenges include operator dependence, probe positioning, and measurement variability, whereas complications of ONSUS are minimal compared with the risks of untreated elevated ICP.</p>
        <p>This educational activity enhances clinician competence in evaluating and managing patients with suspected or confirmed ICP elevation using ONSUS. Participants develop skills in patient selection, procedural technique, and interpretation of optic nerve sheath measurements to guide timely clinical interventions. The instruction emphasizes indications, contraindications, preparation, and the integration of findings into treatment planning. Collaboration with an interprofessional healthcare team, including critical care nurses, neurologists, and surgical staff, improves diagnostic accuracy, supports coordinated care, and promotes optimal patient outcomes through shared decision-making and efficient communication.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify patients presenting with features that warrant assessment using optic nerve sheath ultrasound.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate normal from abnormal optic nerve sheath diameter values across populations.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based, personalized approaches for managing elevated intracranial pressure and preventing potential complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply interprofessional team strategies to improve care coordination and outcomes in patients undergoing&#x000a0;optic nerve sheath ultrasound.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=79614&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=79614">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The use of point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) has expanded considerably over the past 2 decades, enabling rapid evaluations, expedited triage, improved diagnostic capabilities in austere settings, and real-time assessment of focused clinical questions in patients with critical illness in the intensive care unit.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r1">[1]</xref> Emergency medicine clinicians have led efforts to establish and educate clinicians on the use of bedside ultrasound. In 2001, the American College of Emergency Physicians published the first Emergency Ultrasound Guidelines to clarify primary indications, scope of practice, training requirements, and continuing education for emergency ultrasound.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r2">[2]</xref> Checklists, grading criteria, and formal educational recommendations have been published to optimize appropriate use and training in POCUS modalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>These guidelines have continued to expand alongside increasing use of ultrasound. Adoption has extended to multiple other specialties, particularly for procedural ultrasound applications, including real-time guidance for vascular access, peripheral nerve blockade, and presurgical evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;The main distinction between POCUS and dedicated ultrasound examinations lies in the ability to rapidly answer a focused clinical question, facilitate serial assessments during clinical deterioration, or guide a bedside procedure, rather than generate a detailed report with graded pathology.</p>
        <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r7">[7]</xref> The availability of small, portable ultrasound devices has increased access to advanced diagnostics in remote environments and battlefield settings. Physicians and advanced paramedical practitioners in these environments receive training in extended focused assessment with sonography in trauma, optic nerve sheath ultrasound (ONSUS) for evaluation of intracranial hypertension, inferior vena cava collapsibility assessments for volume status, and multiple other image-guided procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r9">[9]</xref> This article focuses on ONSUS for the diagnosis, monitoring, and management of elevated intracranial pressure (ICP).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>The eye provides an ideal configuration for ultrasound imaging due to its superficial location and well-demarcated fluid-filled structures, which serve as excellent media for ultrasound beam transmission (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Labeled Diagram of the Human Eye). A basic understanding of ocular anatomy is necessary to perform ONSUS.&#x000a0;Accurate identification of anterior ocular structures improves the likelihood of assessing the optic nerve sheath in the correct plane.</p>
        <p>The eyeball resides within the Tenon capsule, a membranous sac surrounded by periocular fat, soft tissue, and the orbital walls. Attachments occur via the corneoscleral junction and the optic nerve, while extraocular muscle tendons pierce the Tenon capsule, insert into the sclera, and allow multidirectional eyeball mobility. Two fluid-filled structures that require identification are the anterior and posterior chambers, separated by the lens, both containing anechoic fluid. Posterior to the posterior chamber lies the retina, a hyperechoic nervous membrane.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Behind the retina and optic disc, the optic nerve is identifiable by an anechoic core flanked by a hyperechoic sheath along the medial and lateral borders in the transverse plane. The optic nerve represents an outpouching of intact brain tissue, with the intraorbital segment fully encapsulated by dura, arachnoid, and pia mater. This meningeal covering allows the optic nerve sheath to transmit cerebrospinal fluid and fluctuate in size with changes in ICP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>The meningeal layers of the optic nerve are continuous with the dural and subarachnoid spaces.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r13">[13]</xref> The bulbous portion of the optic nerve, located approximately 3 mm posterior to the globe, demonstrates the greatest distensibility and sensitivity to ICP changes, as observed in <italic toggle="yes">in vivo</italic> and cadaveric models of ICP elevation (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Optic Nerve Sheath Diameter Measurement by Ultrasound).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Normal optic nerve sheath diameter (ONSD) upper limits include 4 mm in infants, 5 mm in children, and 5 mm in adults. ONSD increases linearly up to 7.5 mm, after which measurements appear to plateau.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Ocular ultrasound is indicated for multiple clinical scenarios, including vision loss, headache, and trauma.&#x000a0;This imaging modality&#x000a0;facilitates the identification or exclusion of retinal detachment, foreign bodies, lens dislocation, vitreous hemorrhage, and other superficial abnormalities that may cause vision impairment. ONSUS is typically performed in cases of suspected or confirmed traumatic brain injury, elevated ICP, or optic neuritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;The procedure is noninvasive and avoids the risks of infection, hemorrhage, and ionizing radiation associated with intracranial monitors and computed tomography (CT).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>ONSUS may also assist in ruling out elevated ICP in nonacute trauma settings, such as during troubleshooting malfunctioning intraventricular devices, performing lumbar punctures, or assessing the safety of neuraxial anesthesia in patients with a history of raised ICP. These measurements must be interpreted within the broader clinical context, as such applications remain insufficiently studied and not fully validated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;ONSD is currently considered the most reliable noninvasive modality for dynamic estimation of ICP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r24">[24]</xref> POCUS measurements demonstrate a significant correlation between ONSD and ICP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r25">[25]</xref> ICP&#x000a0;may be estimated using the following formula,&#x000a0;where&#x000a0;nICPONSD is the noninvasively estimated ICP based on ONSD measurement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>nICPONSD = 5.00 &#x000d7; ONSD &#x02212; 13.92 mm Hg</p>
        <p>This technique provides a crucial assessment tool for individuals with critical illness in whom invasive ICP monitoring is unavailable or contraindicated. ONSD evaluation has proven useful across multiple patient cohorts, including individuals with head injury, ischemic stroke, or hematoma progression in hemorrhagic stroke,&#x000a0;patients undergoing neurosurgical procedures, individuals with idiopathic intracranial hypertension or intracranial hypotension, neonates with hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, and patients undergoing shunt evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r38">[38]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Surface ultrasound remains a safe imaging modality with minimal risk in adult patients. In cases of suspected open-globe injury, the procedure carries a risk of further ocular damage and should not be performed without consultation or assistance from an ophthalmologist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r40">[40]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>ONSUS&#x000a0;may be performed with most ultrasound equipment commonly available in emergency departments and intensive care units throughout the United States. Commercially available high-frequency ocular ultrasound units exist for dedicated eye studies, but conventional ultrasound units with high-frequency (7&#x02013;10 MHz or higher) linear array probes are sufficient to visualize the optic nerve using the closed-eye technique.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r42">[42]</xref> Additional materials may include a clear sterile intravenous catheter adhesive to create a barrier and ultrasound gel to serve as a conduction medium.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>ONSUS&#x000a0;is typically&#x000a0;performed by a single operator and requires no additional assistance for patient positioning. In settings with limited space, an assistant experienced in ultrasound may facilitate image capture and optimize settings, such as depth and gain, to enhance imaging quality.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Patients who are awake and alert should receive a briefing on the procedure. Individuals who are obtunded, intubated, or heavily sedated require a gross eye evaluation for signs of globe rupture or hyphema. Evidence of globe rupture, including visible conjunctival or scleral defects, severe chemosis, 360&#x000b0; conjunctival hemorrhage, hypotony, or total hyphema, warrants procedure abortion to prevent further herniation of intraocular contents, as well as consultation with an ophthalmologist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Awake individuals may be instructed to maintain a forward gaze with eyes closed, whereas in obtunded patients, eye orientation should be assessed to align the ultrasound beam in the transverse plane and avoid oblique slices of the optic nerve. Some practitioners apply petroleum jelly or ophthalmic ointment to prevent air entrapment before placement of a transparent plastic adhesive dressing. Ultrasound gel should be applied liberally to the adhesive prior to scanning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Patient positioning should prioritize comfort and examination access, typically in the supine to 30&#x000b0; "head-up" position. Although theoretical concerns exist regarding changes in ICP and ONSD with patient position or ventilator settings, <italic toggle="yes">in vivo</italic> models demonstrate no significant effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r47">[47]</xref> Small studies report stable ONSD measurements despite changes in patient position, positive end-expiratory pressure, or establishment of pneumoperitoneum during laparoscopic procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r48">[48]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r50">[50]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>After placing a sterile dressing over a closed eye and applying a liberal amount of ultrasound gel, a high-frequency ultrasound transducer should be positioned over the eye in the transverse plane, with minimal contact with the dressing. The sonographer&#x02019;s hand should rest on a noncompressible surface, such as the patient&#x02019;s nose, midface, or forehead, to prevent direct pressure on the globe, patient discomfort, and anatomic distortion. Small nasal and temporal movements, followed by caudad and cephalad sweeps, should be performed to capture the optic nerve sheath in the same plane as the anterior chamber, posterior chamber, and lens, thereby avoiding image obliquity. Once the anechoic streak posterior to the optic disc is identified, the image should be captured, and measurements should be obtained from the stored image. The optic nerve is relatively cylindrical and symmetric, theoretically allowing a single view for measurement.</p>
        <p>Nevertheless, the ultrasonographic principle that &#x0201c;one view is no view&#x0201d; should be applied, and an additional image should be acquired in the sagittal plane. After identifying a point 3 mm posterior to the globe, electronic calipers should be used to measure the distance across the area of decreased echogenicity between the hyperechoic margins of the sheath. A large discrepancy between measurements obtained in different planes should raise concern about artifacts or off-axis acquisition and prompt repeat scanning in both planes. Once optimal images are obtained in 2 orthogonal planes, the mean value should be calculated to estimate true ONSD. Both eyes should be examined, and bilateral measurements should be considered.</p>
        <p>If difficulty is encountered while acquiring an image during the ultrasound examination, the focal beam should be centered transversely through the lens plane. The contralateral eye may be assessed to estimate pupil location and orientation in the examined eye. Increasing the volume of the ultrasound gel buffer can improve image quality. Increasing gain may aid in distinguishing the true nerve sheath from the nerve itself. Color Doppler may assist in visualizing the retinal artery near the posterior pole of the globe as an anatomic reference.</p>
        <p>Once the ONSD is measured, findings should be interpreted within the overall clinical context. Early studies most commonly applied a cutoff value of 5.0 mm, demonstrating excellent sensitivity and positive and negative predictive values. However, thresholds correlating with ICP elevations greater than 20 mm Hg have ranged from less than 5 mm to greater than 6 mm across studies. A meta-analysis of 12 studies comparing ONSD measurements with&#x000a0;CT evidence of elevated&#x000a0;ICP reported a specificity of 92.3% and a sensitivity of 95.6% using a 5 mm cutoff in adult patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>A-Mode Versus B-Mode Scanning</bold>
</p>
        <p>A-mode scanning is generally preferred because B-mode imaging is susceptible to blooming artifact.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;However, B-mode scanning allows straightforward determination of the measurement distance along the optic nerve posterior to the globe. B-mode imaging is also easier to perform in noncompliant patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r53">[53]</xref> No significant differences in ONSD measurements have been demonstrated between straight-ahead and lateral gaze during B-mode evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r54">[54]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Point of Optic Nerve Sheath Diameter Measurement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Measurement at 3 mm posterior to the globe corresponds to the bulbous portion of the optic nerve, where pressure-related changes are most pronounced.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a study reported an area under the curve of 0.956 for ONSD measured at this location. Results from other studies have shown no significant difference in ONSD values obtained at 3 mm compared with 5 mm posterior to the globe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r58">[58]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Technique for O<bold>ptic Nerve Sheath Diameter</bold> Measurement</bold>
</p>
        <p>The ONSD assessment&#x000a0;is performed with the patient in the supine position. A high-frequency linear probe, which provides improved contrast between the optic nerve and retrobulbar fat, is placed on the closed upper eyelid after generous application of ultrasound gel (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Ocular Ultrasound Techniques in Transverse and Parasagittal Planes). Acoustic output should be adjusted in accordance with the ALARA (eg, as low as reasonably achievable) principle to minimize exposure to the lens and retina. Probe position is adjusted to visualize the optic nerve as it enters the globe. Measurement is ideally obtained 3 mm posterior to the globe.&#x000a0;The point at which the optic nerve crosses the ophthalmic artery is also recommended. Multiple measurements from each eye should be averaged to reduce variability. Consistent use of the same equipment, technique, acoustic window, contrast, and brightness settings is advised.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r60">[60]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>ONSUS is safe and benign in the absence of an open-globe injury. In cases of suspected open-globe injury, the procedure carries a risk of further ocular damage and should not be performed without consultation or assistance from an ophthalmologist. Although several studies report reasonable sensitivity and specificity, imaging the optic nerve sheath along the traditional visual axis and measuring mean ONSD in the transverse and sagittal planes has been questioned.</p>
        <p>Copetti and colleagues challenge conventional assumptions regarding the optic nerve sheath, suggesting that observed structures may represent artifacts&#x02014;potentially acoustic shadows cast by the lamina cribrosa&#x02014;rather than the optic nerve itself. False-positive measurements caused by artifacts or excessive pressure on the globe could&#x000a0;prompt the&#x000a0;undertaking of unnecessary interventions, invasive monitoring, or additional unindicated studies. Copetti recommends the use of color Doppler to identify the retinal artery and measuring the optic nerve from a lateral approach to improve accuracy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r61">[61]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>ONSUS remains a relatively new modality for ICP assessment. Initial studies indicate that image acquisition is an elementary skill and&#x000a0;may be performed bilaterally in&#x000a0;less than 5 minutes by experienced operators. Research on&#x000a0;skill development for nonphysician Special Operations Combat Medic (SOCM) trainees with minimal or no ultrasound experience demonstrated that, after a 5-minute tutorial, the participants&#x000a0;achieved ONSD measurements statistically comparable to those of expert physicians.</p>
        <p>Additional studies suggest that novice ultrasound users can attain proficiency after approximately 25 scans, whereas experienced sonographers may reach proficiency in as few as 10 scans. Given the safety profile of surface ultrasound and its expanding applicability in both physician and nonphysician training, ONSUS&#x000a0;may&#x000a0;be performed by the entire healthcare team. Results from systematic reviews and meta-analyses indicate that an ONSD of 5 to 5.70 mm is associated with ICP above 20 mm Hg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r62">[62]</xref>&#x000a0;The mean ONSD was reported as 5.82 mm across the included studies.</p>
        <p>The ratio of the ONSD to the eyeball transverse diameter (ETD), with a mean value of 0.18, demonstrates greater reliability than ONSD measurement alone. This&#x000a0;finding&#x000a0;is supported by the following factors:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Significant correlation between ONSD and ETD</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Overlap of standard deviations between normal and pathological ONSD values</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Independence from demographic variables, including sex, height, weight, and body mass index</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>ONSD responds to ICP in real time. The likelihood of plastic deformation or overdistention of the optic nerve sheath is minimal within the effective measurement range.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r63">[63]</xref> The ONSD&#x02013;ETD ratio serves as a practical adjunct to other standard diagnostic modalities. However, standardized methods and established reference ranges are required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r64">[64]</xref> Consensus statements advocating ONSD monitoring as a noninvasive surrogate for ICP recommend a quality-assurance checklist that encompasses probe selection, patient safety, positioning, image acquisition, and measurement techniques.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r65">[65]</xref></p>
        <p>ONSUS has broad applications in both advanced medical centers and battlefield settings. Transport of critically ill individuals from the intensive care unit (ICU) to radiology suites is often challenging and potentially life-threatening. Mortality during interhospital transfer of individuals with critical illness has been reported at up to 2%, with adverse event rates reaching 68%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r66">[66]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from several studies indicate that CT scans produce therapeutic changes in only approximately 39% to 68% of cases, supporting the judicious use of ICU radiographic studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r68">[68]</xref></p>
        <p>Head CT accounts for more than 25% of ICU interhospital transports, and, depending on the clinical indication, ONSUS may serve as a modality to guide decision-making regarding the need for such imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r69">[69]</xref> In a&#x000a0;patient&#x000a0;with multisystem traumatic injuries,&#x000a0;a known, stable intracranial hemorrhage, and established baseline ONSD, neurological decline in the ICU may justify serial ONSUS to evaluate for intracranial hypertension or herniation when transport carries significant risk. Hemorrhage progression without intracranial hypertension would not alter management. Therefore, early and frequent ONSUS in patients at risk for increased ICP is critical, as significant changes in ONSD are more indicative of ICP fluctuations than a single absolute measurement.</p>
        <p>Ultrasound has been employed by clinicians in diverse environments, including high-altitude, polar, battlefield, and in-flight settings, where other imaging modalities are limited or unavailable. Traumatic brain injury (TBI) is a prevalent battlefield injury, with over 200,000 cases reported by the Department of Defense between 2010 and 2018. More than 80% of these TBIs are classified as mild and do not require neurosurgical intervention, yet evaluation in austere locations can be difficult.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r70">[70]</xref></p>
        <p>ONSUS enables rapid triage and minimally invasive evaluation of intracranial hypertension in equivocal clinical settings.&#x000a0;This imaging modality provides actionable data to guide early medical treatment while facilitating timely evacuation to definitive neurosurgical care. Although ONSUS is not specifically referenced in the most recent Joint Trauma System Clinical Practice Guideline on Traumatic Brain/Severe Head Injury (2 March 2017), the guideline emphasizes that &#x0201c;treatment of known or suspected intracranial hypertension remains a cornerstone of therapy in patients with severe brain injury.&#x0201d;</p>
        <p>Recommended conservative management includes head-of-bed elevation to 30&#x000b0; to 45&#x000b0;, systolic blood pressure greater than 100 to 110 mm Hg, normoventilation with&#x000a0;a&#x000a0;partial pressure of arterial carbon dioxide&#x000a0;(PaCO2) of 35 to 40 mm Hg, blood glucose below 180 mg/dL, PaO2 above 60 mm Hg, and seizure prophylaxis. Treatment with 3% saline targeting a serum sodium of 150 to 160 mEq/L should be initiated if no contraindications exist. A treatment goal of less than 22 mm Hg is commonly cited when invasive ICP monitoring is employed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r71">[71]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r72">[72]</xref>&#x000a0;Invasive ICP monitors require neurosurgical expertise, and traditional ONSD cutoffs of 5 mm generally correlate with ICP above 20 mm Hg. ONSUS may serve as an adjunct for determining the initiation of hypertonic therapy and real-time monitoring of treatment effects.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A positive 30&#x000b0; test, involving ONSD assessment in primary gaze and at 30&#x000b0; from primary gaze, differentiates ICP elevation due to fluid overload from infiltrative pathologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r73">[73]</xref> The &#x0201c;crescent sign&#x0201d; is observed in chronic ICP elevations compared with acute forms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r74">[74]</xref>&#x000a0;Although invasive methods are considered the gold standard for ICP measurement, they carry significant risks of infection and hemorrhage, are not universally available, and incur substantial costs, particularly in low- and middle-income countries. ONSUS offers a bedside, noninvasive, portable, rapid, reproducible, radiation-free, inexpensive, and easily performed alternative with minimal learning requirements.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r75">[75]</xref></p>
        <p>Competence in ONSUS&#x000a0;may be achieved without formal radiology training. Pocket-sized ultrasound devices further enhance accessibility and practicality in diverse clinical settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r76">[76]</xref>&#x000a0;Several limitations affect the use of ONSUS. The technique is operator-dependent, and measurement accuracy may be influenced by the clinical setting, such as the emergency department versus the ICU.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r77">[77]</xref> Meta-analyses have demonstrated a wide range of diagnostic odds ratios for detecting raised ICP, reflecting variability in study design and measurement methodology.</p>
        <p>Anatomically, the optic nerve sheath is not perfectly circular and constitutes a complex system of arachnoid trabeculae, pillars, and septa that compartmentalize the subarachnoid space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r78">[78]</xref> ONSD expansion may persist even after ICP normalization due to impaired sheath retraction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-79614.r79">[79]</xref> In addition to ICP, multiple physiologic and anatomic factors influence ONSD <italic toggle="yes">in vivo</italic>. Variables such as eyeball movement, axial length, myopia, orbital or intraocular lesions, and the configuration of the optic canal within the sphenoid bone can alter measurements and affect interpretation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>POCUS has become an essential tool for rapid bedside evaluation, triage, and monitoring of individuals with critical illness.&#x000a0;The use of this imaging tool has expanded across emergency, critical care, perioperative, and austere settings, enabling clinicians to answer focused clinical questions in real time. ONSUS is a noninvasive POCUS application that permits estimation of ICP, providing crucial information in patients&#x000a0;experiencing traumatic or neurological disorders or undergoing perioperative care.&#x000a0;ONSD measurement&#x000a0;allows&#x000a0;the detection of ICP elevation, guidance of early interventions, and reduction of reliance on invasive monitoring or high-risk transport for imaging studies. ONSUS provides rapid, reproducible, and safe assessments that support timely decision-making and may improve patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>Competence in ONSUS requires an interprofessional approach. Physicians and advanced practitioners perform accurate assessments, interpret results, and integrate findings into care plans. Nurses facilitate patient positioning, monitoring, and procedural safety, while pharmacists ensure appropriate pharmacologic management of ICP. Effective interprofessional communication and coordination enable timely interventions, serial monitoring, and shared decision-making, promoting patient-centered care, optimizing outcomes, and enhancing team performance in high-acuity and resource-limited environments.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s13" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>In the appropriate clinical setting, ONSUS can provide sufficient information to initiate neuroprotective strategies or medical management while awaiting further imaging, evacuation, or neurosurgical intervention. All members of the healthcare team should understand the implications of an elevated or rising ONSD and be familiar with ICP-lowering interventions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-79614.s14" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring</title>
        <p>ONSUS training is suitable for a wide range of healthcare professionals, including those with minimal prior ultrasound experience. In many centers providing neurosurgical services, a neurosurgeon is not available in-house at all times. Therefore, initial evaluation and interim monitoring of patients are frequently the responsibility of emergency medicine, intensive care, and nursing clinicians. Teaching this modality to all team members who commonly care for patients with neurosurgical conditions or critical illness offers substantial clinical utility.</p>
      </sec>
